cription\": \"URL validation accepts empty strings as valid. While intentional for optional fields, this could mask misconfigurations or allow open redirect vulnerabilities if used for redirect URLs.\",\n      \"category\": \"insufficient-validation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/api.ts\",\n      \"line\": 42,\n      \"description\": \"Token cleared on 401 response but no explicit redirect to login page. Users are left in unclear state, potentially seeing broken UI or stale data.\",\n      \"category\": \"incomplete-logout\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/api.ts\",\n      \"line\": 1,\n      \"description\": \"API base URL defaults to http://localhost:8000 if VITE_API_BASE is not set. Could cause production API requests to go to localhost if environment variable is missing.\",\n      \"category\": \"misconfiguration\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/utils/errors.ts\",\n      \"line\": 27,\n      \"description\": \"Detailed error messages expose backend infrastructure details (FastAPI, Mongo, LiveKit). This information helps attackers understand the stack and target specific vulnerabilities.\",\n      \"category\": \"information-disclosure\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"riskScore\": 72,\n  \"recommendations\": [\n    \"CRITICAL: Migrate token storage from localStorage to httpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict cookies. This prevents XSS attacks from stealing tokens and provides automatic CSRF protection for state-changing requests.\",\n    \"CRITICAL: Implement CSRF token protection by: (1) generating tokens on backend, (2) sending as cookie + request header/body, (3) validating on backend for state-changing requests (POST, PUT, DELETE).\",\n    \"HIGH: Sanitize all error messages before displaying to users. Use DOMPurify or similar library to strip HTML/scripts. Only display safe, user-facing error messages; log detailed errors server-side.\",\n    \"HIGH: Add Content Security Policy headers via vite config or backend. Start with: `default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:;`\",\n    \"HIGH: Implement explicit login redirect on 401 response. Redirect to /login, clear all app state, and show a session-expired message.\",\n    \"MEDIUM: Encrypt sensitive cached data in localStorage or migrate to sessionStorage (cleared on tab close). Use TweetNaCl.js or libsodium.js for encryption.\",\n    \"MEDIUM: Reduce error message verbosity in production. Don't expose backend framework names, services (Mongo, LiveKit), or internal paths. Use generic errors like 'Service unavailable, please try again later'.\",\n    \"MEDIUM: Add additional security headers: X-Frame-Options: DENY (prevent clickjacking), X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin.\",\n    \"MEDIUM: Implement Rate Limiting on authentication endpoints (backend) to prevent credential stuffing and brute force attacks.\",\n    \"LOW: Use environment variable validation in CI/CD to ensure VITE_API_BASE is always set for production builds. Add a build-time check that fails if missing.\",\n    \"LOW: Consider implementing Subresource Integrity (SRI) for any CDN-hosted dependencies to prevent supply chain attacks.\",\n    \"LOW: Add security logging for sensitive operations (token access, auth failures, unusual API calls) for audit trails and anomaly detection.\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n**Key Findings:**\n- **Token storage in localStorage** is the most critical issue—any XSS attack can steal the Bearer token and impersonate the user\n- **Missing CSRF protection** allows attackers to trigger state-changing operations (delete bots, modify campaigns) from other sites\n- **Unfiltered error messages** expose backend infrastructure and could enable XSS attacks\n- **No security headers** make the application an easier target for browser-based attacks\n\n**Immediate Action Items:**\n1. Migrate from localStorage to httpOnly cookies (requires backend coordination)\n2. Add CSRF token validation to all state-changing requests\n3. Sanitize error messages and reduce information disclosure\n4. Configure CSP and other security headers in Vite or backend\n\nThe risk score of **72/100** reflects a moderately high-risk application—the primary exposure is to XSS and CSRF attacks, which are common and impactful in modern web applications.\n",
